<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> No ( Already have a successful article )</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> I never know what to put here.</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> What if the Yakuza was secretly lead by a highly capable virtual Japanese popstar.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;This&nbsp;is&nbsp;a&nbsp;weird&nbsp;one</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The leader of one of the Yakuza families has a daughter who is terminally I'll. He uses all of his influence and power to download her conciousness into an anomalous virtual world that is technicaly superior to any others so that she could continue to live. The daughter has a dream of being a famous popstar, so he provides her a virtual stage to sing and dance on. Videos are posted online of her performances, and she quickly becomes a well known popstar in Japan. The Yakuza uses this new platform to draw the young people into joining the Yakuza. Time passes and the leader of the Yakuza dies, leaving no &quot;living&quot; heirs. Various members of the Yakuza fight to become the new head, but things start to go wrong when those who try to take power are assassinated. It is revealed that the virtual popstar believes that she is the true leader of the Yakuza, and that she has used her new virtual conciousness to grow her intelligence greatly. The members attempting to take power are unable to get rid of her due to the old boss hiding the server containing the popstar, and the virtual world. Through blood, blackmail, and J-pop, the virtual popstar manages to unite the Yakuza families, and become the leader of the largest criminal syndicate in not only Japan, but the entire world.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> An article that portrays the virtual popstar as a serious threat to normalcy, and the safety of the Japanese people.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Okay&#8230; This idea is absurd. I am looking to find out how the community would feel about this article as well if something like this has been done before. ( I doubt it has. ) I will also note that I am not a fan of J-pop, and that this idea came from a random joke my brother and I came up with. Let me know what you think!</p>
</div>
</div>
