														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842290">
									

<p>Hey, thanks for the crit (and thanks for the greenlight)!</p>
<p>I'm just going to clarify some things, hopefully they clear up your concerns:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open&nbsp;Clarification&nbsp;+</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;-</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I…think my biggest concern about this idea is that it feels too much like it's pulling back the curtain around pattern screamers to reveal that they're just generic murder monsters: this feels like it'd be stronger if everything was almost exactly as it is now except there's no entity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah, I really didn't have much description about this 'entity' I mentioned (despite the plot revolving almost entirely around it), so I'll clear it, and its motives, up now.</p>
<p>The entity itself is the non-existent manifestation (I know this is an oxymoron) of the very existent hatred of the non-existent pattern screamers. Its 'form' isn't actually seen in this tale, in fact, it doesn't really have a form (being non-existent). My best attempt at describing it is like a barely perceivable 'wrinkle' (our brain filling in the gap that is this entity to its best extent) in the space in front of us, or the reflection of an invisible being in a warped mirror.</p>
<p>Now for its motives. The entity, being the non-existent manifestation of very real hate, is simply a vessel for the pattern screamers' malice and rage against those who have given them existence. It kills not because it can, but because its very non-existent existence is torment, and, like the pattern screamers, it <em>hates</em>. Like a terrified wild animal that lashes out against the people trying to help it, it lashes out against the scientists trying to perceive it. Once there are only ten people aware again, the hate (from the pattern screamers) fuelling it loses its potency, and it proceeds to bolt back to where it came from (this is what I meant by it 'disappearing': its presence was no longer felt).</p>
<p>The thing is, this creature is intelligent. Like the pattern screamers who birthed it, it can communicate and understand language<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-41134-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-41134-1')">1</a></sup> (in this case, Russian, like the soldiers who were sent into the void and never returned). Throughout the tale, I'll have it scrawl things like 'I'm scared', 'STOP LOOKING AT ME', and 'Why are you doing this to me?' into the walls and floors of the facility. This should really work to up the 'creep' factor of this tale, which I intend to have mainly consist of paranoia and psychological horror (e.g scientists looking into an empty room and not knowing whether the creature is there or not. Any deaths will be completely out of the blue).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>And that's about it for my clarification. Thanks for reading, and I hope this clears some things up!</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-41134-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-41134-1')">1</a>. Evidence of this is in the exploration log, where a pattern screamer tells the D-Class to lean back. My theory is that the pattern screamers share a hive mind of information and knowledge from its victims, and this is how the entity can communicate and understand why it is suffering.</div>
</div>

