<blockquote>
<p>IMO: this would be easier to write if the SCP was the ancient spacecraft; and then we worked backwards from the notes on the spacecraft to figure out what happened.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's actually&#8230; a much better idea. Thank you.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Or well, actually, if I was to give advice on writing this, my suggestion would be: really, strongly consider whether this would be best as a tale from the perspective of the crew.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, so I've thought about doing as a tale. But I'm still not totally sure on how writing a tale actually works. Does it need to be on an existing SCP? Can they be completely standalone?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>It might just need a little bit more narrative heft to it: perhaps there's question of like, where the Einstein-Rosen bridge came from that could have weight if answered, though it may be hard to not end up making something which feels very Mass Effect Reaper like if you do that.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I was going to leave up somewhat up to interpretation. One of the crew, for example, suggests it was God that created the wormhole. She cites the fact that the wormhole took 7 days to fully manifest, drawing parallels to Genesis in the Bible. Another member of the crew, however, states he thinks it was created by future humans (or perhaps even a future incarnation of the Foundation), who know about the event in the present, and therefore created the wormhole in the past to facilitate humanity creating themselves. The fact that the wormhole collapsed directly after they seeded the primordial Earth would support this, given the fact that interfering with the primordial Earth too much could lead to humanity never existing in the first place. Kind of like a paradox I guess?</p>
<p>Hahah, don't worry. There won't be any extra-galactic space cuttlefish that have come to purge the galaxy of sapient life.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm…really kinda hesitant on this exclusively because the idea feels…somewhat common? It's a relatively cliche time loop story where we seed our own planet that's somewhat reminiscent of stuff like Interstellar but on a bit of a larger scale; and it feels like this really needs some kind of unique spin on this beyond just the foundation's involvement to make it more interesting to a reader.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I honestly wasn't aware that humanity creating itself was a common trope. Do you have any suggestions that could make it more unique?</p>
